.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library is the CUDA Runtime API of this project: the entry points a CUDA
program calls, answered from the Driver API of
.Xr libcuda 3 .
It uses neither the Linux compatibility layer nor NVIDIA's CUDA libraries.
The public header is
.In cuda_runtime_api.h .
The declarations carry no execution-space attribute: a function declared
without one is a host function, which is what every entry point here is.
All exported functions have the provisional symbol version
.Dv CUDART_1.0 .
.Pp
The library is installed as
.Dv libcudart-native.so.13
with that soname.
The soname is not
.Dv libcudart.so.13 ,
because the SDK tree ships a link-only stub under that name for the
toolchain: a program that links against one of the two must never load the
other.
The directory that holds the runtime also holds a
.Dv libcudart.so
link, so that
.Fl lcudart
finds the runtime there; the soname of the library stays
.Dv libcudart-native.so.13 .
.Ss The driver underneath
The runtime initializes the driver once per process, the first time a call
needs it.
The driver chooses its provider with
.Ev CUDA_NATIVE_BACKEND ,
and the synthetic provider takes the profiles of
.Ev CUDA_NATIVE_FAKE_PROFILE ;
see
.Xr libcuda 3
for the providers, the profiles and the visibility filter.
When the driver does not start, every call that needs it reports the
driver's failure and the reason of it.
.Ss Devices and the primary context
.Fn cudaGetDeviceCount
reports the devices the driver exposes to this process.
.Fn cudaGetDevice
reports the device of the calling thread; the first device is the default
of a thread that has not named one, and the primary context of that device
is retained like the context of any other device.
.Fn cudaSetDevice
makes a device the device of the calling thread: the runtime retains the
primary context of that device, the one the driver holds for it, and makes
it the current context of the thread.
The retained context belongs to the device and not to a thread, so two
threads that use the same device share one context.
.Fn cudaDeviceReset
releases the primary context the runtime retained for the device of the
calling thread.
.Pp
.Fn cudaGetDeviceProperties
fills the properties of a device from the driver: the name, the total
memory, the shared memory per block, the largest block, the warp size, the
number of multiprocessors, the compute capability, whether the device is
integrated and the PCI identity.
A property the driver does not report stays at zero.
The structure is this SDK's: a program built against another SDK must not
exchange one with a program built against this one.
.Ss Streams and events
The default stream is the stream of handle zero, and it is the only stream
a caller may name: the runtime holds no stream of its own, so any other
handle is
.Dv cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle .
The runtime holds no event at all, so every event handle is
.Dv cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle .
Creating a stream or an event is refused with
.Dv cudaErrorNotSupported ,
because the driver cannot create one yet.
.Ss What the driver cannot do yet
A call that needs a driver entry point the driver does not have reports
.Dv cudaErrorNotSupported
and names the driver call that is missing.
It never reports a success it did not achieve.
The reason is readable with
.Fn cudaNativeLastFailureReason ,
and it names the driver call, for example
.Dq cuMemAlloc
for
.Fn cudaMalloc .
An argument the runtime can reject on its own is rejected before the
driver is asked: a null output pointer is
.Dv cudaErrorInvalidValue ,
and an output the runtime does not change on failure keeps what it had.
Releasing a null pointer is the one operation that succeeds without the
driver:
.Fn cudaFree
and
.Fn cudaFreeHost
answer
.Dv cudaSuccess
for a null pointer, because there is nothing to release.
.Pp
A copy between two host addresses is the runtime's own work:
.Fn cudaMemcpy
with
.Dv cudaMemcpyHostToHost
copies the bytes and reports
.Dv cudaSuccess .
Every other direction needs the driver and is refused.
.Ss The error of a thread
The error state of the runtime is per thread.
A call that fails records its error and the reason of it in the calling
thread.
.Fn cudaGetLastError
reports that error and clears it;
.Fn cudaPeekAtLastError
reports it and keeps it.
A successful call does not clear the error of the thread.
The reason of the failure goes with the error: reading the error with
.Fn cudaGetLastError
takes the reason away as well.
The two error queries,
.Fn cudaGetErrorName
and
.Fn cudaGetErrorString ,
answer about a code and leave the error state of the thread alone.
An unknown code is
.Dv cudaErrorInvalidValue
and the output is set to null.
.Ss The registration contract of clang's CUDA mode
The generated code of a CUDA module registers its fat binary with
.Fn __cudaRegisterFatBinary ,
registers every kernel of it with
.Fn __cudaRegisterFunction ,
and ends the registration with
.Fn __cudaRegisterFatBinaryEnd .
The runtime keeps the registrations: the host stub of a kernel, the
pointer a launch names, and the device name the fat binary gives the
kernel.
A launch is answered by looking its host stub up in that table.
The configuration of a launch is pushed with
.Fn __cudaPushCallConfiguration
and popped with
.Fn __cudaPopCallConfiguration ;
it is per thread, so two threads that launch at the same time have their
own configuration.
.Pp
The runtime does not load the images of a fat binary: the driver cannot
load a module yet.
A launch of a registered kernel therefore stops at that limit, reports
.Dv cudaErrorNotSupported ,
and names the driver call that is missing.
A launch of a host stub that names no registered kernel is
.Dv cudaErrorInvalidResourceHandle .
.Ss The cuda-native extensions
.Fn cudaNativeLastFailureReason
returns the reason of the last failure of the calling thread, or null when
the thread has no failure to report.
.Fn cudaNativeKernelCount
returns the number of kernels the registration contract registered.
.Fn cudaNativeKernelName
writes the device name of the registered kernel at the given index into
the caller's buffer, whole or not at all, and returns nonzero when it
wrote the whole of it.
.Fn cudaNativeKernelHostStub
writes the device name of the kernel the given host stub belongs to, whole
or not at all.
.Fn cudaNativeFatBinaryCount
returns the number of fat binaries the registration contract registered.
.Fn cudaNativeFatBinaryMembers
writes the number of members of the fat binary at the given index and
returns nonzero when it wrote it.
.Fn cudaNativeRuntimeVersion
returns the release of the runtime as major * 10000 + minor * 100 + patch,
and
.Fn cudaNativeRuntimeVersionString
returns the same release as text.
The two come from the one macro of the driver header, so the number and
the text cannot disagree.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions return
.Dv cudaSuccess
on success or a
.Vt cudaError_t
error value.
Unless documented otherwise, output pointers must not be null and are not
changed on failure.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The runtime reads no environment variable of its own.
The provider, the profiles and the visibility filter are the driver's, and
they are described in
.Xr libcuda 3 .
